Model weights
-------------
This repository contains no model weights, with one exception: the 80
band-pass filters of the voice cloning speaker network's SincNet front end
(sincnet.conv1d.0.filters in tools/kt2n_assets/clone_sincnet_reference.json,
with the fixed filterbank buffers n_ and window_) are computed from the learned
cut-off frequencies of pyannote/embedding (below; MIT, Copyright (c) 2022
CNRS), so they are derived from its weights. tools/prepare_assets.py downloads
the weights and converts them into a local model directory. Converted files are
derived from these weights and remain subject to their licenses.

- KittenML/kitten-tts-2 on Hugging Face (latest revision): speech language
  model, tokenizer and built-in voices. Licensed under the Stellon Labs
  Community License (shown on Hugging Face as license "other"):
  https://huggingface.co/KittenML/kitten-tts-2/blob/main/LICENSE.md
  Read it before using or distributing the weights or files converted from
  them. Among other terms, it asks anyone who distributes the model materials
  to keep this notice in a "Notice" text file: "This Stellon Labs Model is
  licensed under the Stellon Labs Community License, Copyright © Stellon Labs
  Inc. All Rights Reserved".
- ResembleAI/chatterbox-turbo on Hugging Face (revision 1e4698ca, file
  s3gen_meanflow.safetensors): speech decoder weights. The model card declares
  the MIT license.
- pyannote/embedding (revision 4db48997), only for optional voice cloning.
  Preparation reads its weights from speaker/model.safetensors in
  KittenML/kitten-tts-2 (no login needed). The model card and that folder's
  licence file declare the MIT license, Copyright (c) 2022 CNRS; see
  licenses/pyannote-embedding.txt.


Speech decoder
--------------
The CUDA encoder, MeanFlow estimator and HiFT vocoder (src/render/,
include/kt2n/render/) and the decoder conversion (tools/kt2n_assets/) are a
port of the S3Gen decoder in Chatterbox by Resemble AI:
https://github.com/resemble-ai/chatterbox
MIT, Copyright (c) 2025 Resemble AI; see licenses/Chatterbox.txt.
That decoder is itself built from:
- CosyVoice, https://github.com/FunAudioLLM/CosyVoice
  Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2024 Alibaba Inc; see licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.
  Its transformer components carry Apache-2.0 notices from ESPnet
  (Copyright (c) 2019 Shigeki Karita; Copyright (c) 2020 Johns Hopkins
  University) and WeNet (Copyright (c) 2020-2021 Mobvoi Inc.).
- Matcha-TTS, https://github.com/shivammehta25/Matcha-TTS
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2023 Shivam Mehta; see licenses/Matcha-TTS.txt.
  Its transformer blocks use Hugging Face diffusers (Apache-2.0).


Voice cloning
-------------
The optional cloning networks (src/voice/) and their preparation tools
(tools/kt2n_assets/clone_assets.py, export_clone.py) are ports of:
- the pyannote.audio speaker-embedding network (SincNet front end and x-vector)
  used by pyannote/embedding: https://github.com/pyannote/pyannote-audio
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2020 CNRS; see licenses/pyannote-audio.txt.
- the CAM++ speaker encoder from 3D-Speaker,
  https://github.com/modelscope/3D-Speaker (Apache-2.0; see
  licenses/Apache-2.0.txt), in the form adapted by FunASR
  (https://github.com/modelscope/FunASR, MIT; see licenses/FunASR.txt) and
  included in Chatterbox's S3Gen.
- the S3Tokenizer speech tokenizer, https://github.com/xingchensong/S3Tokenizer
  Apache-2.0; see licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.
Front-end constants (Kaldi filter banks, mel bases, windows and resampling
kernels) and the speaker network's SincNet filterbank were computed once with
torchaudio (BSD-2-Clause), librosa (ISC), SciPy (BSD-3-Clause) and
asteroid-filterbanks (MIT), and ship as reference values in
tools/kt2n_assets/ (clone_consts_reference.json, clone_sincnet_reference.json).
Those packages are used only by the maintainers' export tool
(tools/export_clone_assets.py) and are not included here.

The decoder conversion's weight-norm fold (tools/kt2n_assets/weight_norm.py)
evaluates, in NumPy, the same floating-point operations as PyTorch's CUDA
weight-norm kernel (aten/src/ATen/native/cuda/WeightNorm.cu, BSD-3-Clause),
so that it reproduces the weights folded with PyTorch; that file itself
contains no PyTorch code.


PyTorch kernels reproduced in the engine
----------------------------------------
So that the engine's results match the reference model bit for bit, some of
its GPU code is written after PyTorch 2.7.1's CUDA kernels (BSD-3-Clause):
- the LayerNorm in src/cuda/kernels/ops_spk.cu follows
  aten/src/ATen/native/cuda/layer_norm_kernel.cu (vectorized_layer_norm_kernel
  and its Welford helpers compute_stats, cuWelfordOnlineSum,
  cuWelfordCombine), with the same thread layout and order of operations;
- the sampling noise (src/cuda/kernels/samp_philox.cuh), the sampler's
  softmax block (src/cuda/kernels/samp_sampler.cu) and a scan in the vocoder
  (src/render/hift/hift.cu) follow the launch geometry and arithmetic of
  PyTorch's exponential_, softmax and scan kernels.
This code is compiled into the CUDA and HIP engine libraries (both Python
engine wheels). PyTorch's copyright notice and license: licenses/PyTorch.txt.


Text normalization
------------------
The normalization token parser in src/text/nemo.cc is derived from NVIDIA
NeMo Text Processing (nemo_text_processing/text_normalization/token_parser.py,
Apache-2.0; see licenses/Apache-2.0.txt):
Copyright (c) 2021, NVIDIA CORPORATION. All rights reserved.
https://github.com/NVIDIA/NeMo-text-processing
KittenML ported it to C++ and changed it to run on the engine's OpenFst
graphs.
The detokenizer in src/text/nemo.cc ports the English MosesDetokenizer of
sacremoses 0.2.0, and the Moses character classes kMosesIsAlpha and kMosesIsSc
in src/text/unicode_tables.inc (perluniprops) are exported from sacremoses
0.2.0 (MIT License; see licenses/Sacremoses.txt):
Copyright (c) 2020 alvations
https://github.com/alvations/sacremoses
This code is compiled into the engine library (both Python engine wheels and
every source build with text normalization).
Preparation reads English word lists from the nemo_text_processing 1.2.0 wheel
on PyPI (Apache-2.0; read as data, never installed) and English grammar data
from KittenML/kitten-text-processing at commit 1988d61 (Apache-2.0):
https://github.com/KittenML/kitten-text-processing
Grammar generation and word lists retain their original NeMo notices and
source identifiers in the asset manifest.

OpenFst 1.8.3 is distributed under Apache-2.0; see licenses/OpenFst.txt.
The build helper repairs two template copy-operation typos for newer GCC.
PCRE2 10.42 is distributed under its BSD license; see licenses/PCRE2.txt.

The optional multilingual normalization tables retain their Unicode license,
notice and source manifest in src/text/voice/. Unicode 14 character tables
are generated from Python's Unicode database. Tokenizer character classes
follow Hugging Face tokenizers 0.22.2 (Apache-2.0).
The generated character-category and normalization tables retain these
licenses in licenses/Python.txt, licenses/Unicode.txt and licenses/Tokenizers.txt.
They are compact tables extracted from the named versions; they do not
include a Python interpreter or the tokenizers runtime.

src/voice/consent_phrase.cc also contains a C++ port of CPython's
difflib.SequenceMatcher (used for the voice-cloning consent-phrase check),
under the Python Software Foundation License retained in licenses/Python.txt.
Summary of the changes made to Python (PSF License section 3): the pure-Python
class is rewritten in C++ over byte strings; only __chain_b (with autojunk),
find_longest_match, get_matching_blocks and ratio are ported; isjunk is not
supported (always None); the public SequenceMatcher API, the quick-ratio
shortcuts and Differ/HtmlDiff are omitted.


