Microsoft Visual C++ runtime
----------------------------
The Windows build of kitten-inference-tts2-cu12 links the static Microsoft
Visual C++ runtime (CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY=MultiThreaded, /MT) into
kt2n_engine.dll, kitten-tts-native.exe and kt2n_prepare_text_data.exe, so
they need no Visual C++ redistributable. That code is distributed under
Microsoft's licence terms for distributable code
(https://learn.microsoft.com/visualstudio/releases/2026/redistribution).
Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation.

NVIDIA CUDA runtime
-------------------
The NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit is installed separately for builds. The CUDA build
(the default build and the kitten-inference-tts2-cu12 package) links
CUDA::cudart_static and uses CMAKE_CUDA_RUNTIME_LIBRARY=Static, so its
shared library contains NVIDIA CUDA runtime code. That code retains
NVIDIA's terms; it is not Apache-2.0 material. CUDA 12.8's EULA lists
libcudart_static.a among redistributable components, subject to its distribution
requirements. Review those terms before distributing binaries:
https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/12.8.0/eula/index.html

The CUDA build also compiles cuRAND's device functions from the CUDA
Toolkit's headers (curand_kernel.h: curand_init, curand_normal4, used by the
vocoder in src/render/hift/hift.cu) into its GPU code. They are NVIDIA
software under the same EULA. Their Philox4x32-10 generator
(curand_philox4x32_x.h) also carries D. E. Shaw Research's Random123 notice,
which licenses/Random123.txt reproduces.

Philox4x32-10 is the counter-based generator of J. K. Salmon, M. A. Moraes,
R. O. Dror and D. E. Shaw, "Parallel random numbers: as easy as 1, 2, 3"
(SC '11), from the Random123 library. src/cuda/kernels/samp_philox.cuh (both
builds), include/kt2n/gpu_rand.h (HIP build, on rocRAND) and
tests/gpu/philox_ref.h (tests) implement it and the Box-Muller transform with
the stream layout of cuRAND's device API, so that a seed gives the same
numbers in every build.

The ROCm (HIP) build links no NVIDIA library and does not use cuRAND: its
random numbers come from rocRAND and include/kt2n/gpu_rand.h (above). See the
next section.


AMD ROCm (HIP build)
--------------------
ROCm is installed separately for builds. The HIP build (cmake --preset hip,
and the kitten-inference-tts2-rocm package) compiles code from these ROCm
components into its shared library:
- rocRAND, https://github.com/ROCm/rocRAND: the Philox4x32-10 generator
  (rocrand_init, rocrand4), used through include/kt2n/gpu_rand.h in GPU code.
  MIT, Copyright (C) Advanced Micro Devices, Inc.; see
  licenses/rocm/rocRAND.txt.
- HIP, https://github.com/ROCm/hip and https://github.com/ROCm/clr: inline
  functions from the HIP headers. MIT, Copyright (C) Advanced Micro Devices,
  Inc.; see licenses/rocm/HIP.txt.
- ROCm Device Libraries (amd/device-libs in
  https://github.com/ROCm/llvm-project): the GPU math and runtime functions
  that the HIP compiler links into every GPU code object. University of
  Illinois/NCSA Open Source License, Copyright (c) 2014-2016, Advanced Micro
  Devices, Inc.; see licenses/rocm/ROCm-Device-Libs.txt.
These texts are the ones the ROCm 7.1 packages ship (the version the
kitten-inference-tts2-rocm package is built with). The ROCm runtime itself
(libamdhip64.so.7 and the libraries it loads) is not included: the HIP build
uses the ROCm 7.x installed on the system.


FFmpeg libraries
----------------
Optional output encoders (KT2N_AV) and reference-audio decoding for cloning
(KT2N_VOICE_AV) link FFmpeg's libavformat, libavcodec, libswresample and libavutil.
The enabled FFmpeg build's own license and configuration apply; some builds
include GPL components. Review the actual linked libraries before distributing
a cloning-enabled shared library. See https://ffmpeg.org/legal.html
FFmpeg source is not bundled here.


Other dependencies
------------------
Python preparation dependencies (NumPy, huggingface_hub, tokenizers; PyTorch
only for the optional --weight-norm-device cuda|cpu; PyTorch, torchaudio,
SciPy, librosa and asteroid-filterbanks only for the maintainers' export tool)
and GoogleTest (tests only) retain their upstream licenses; their source is
not included here and none of them is needed at inference time. Source revisions for downloaded files are
